## Mean-field Hubbard model
Our model Hamiltonian for the MOF-substrate system is
$$
\hat{H}=\hat{H}_{0}+\hat{H}_{\mathsf{U}}+\hat{H}_{\mathsf{s u b}}+\hat{H}_{\mathsf{c o u p l e}}.
$$
The first two terms are for the kagome lattice, describing nearest-neighbour hopping and on-site Coulomb interactions respectively44, defined as
$$
\hat{H}_{0}=-t\sum_{\langle i j\rangle,\sigma}\hat{c}_{i,\sigma}^{\dagger}\hat{c}_{j,\sigma}+\varepsilon,
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\hat{H}_{\mathsf{U}}=U_{\mathsf{H}}\sum_{i}\hat{n}_{i,\uparrow}\hat{n}_{i,\downarrow}}\\ &{\quad\approx U_{\mathsf{H}}\sum_{i}\big(\hat{n}_{i,\uparrow}\langle\hat{n}_{i,\downarrow}\rangle+\hat{n}_{i,\downarrow}\langle\hat{n}_{i,\uparrow}\rangle-\langle\hat{n}_{i,\uparrow}\rangle\langle\hat{n}_{i,\downarrow}\rangle\big),}\end{array}
$$
where $t$ is the hopping constant, $\varepsilon$ is the on-site energy, $U_{\mathsf{H}}$ is the Coulomb interaction, $\hat{c}_{i,\sigma}^{\dag}~(\hat{c}_{i,\sigma})$ creates (annihilates) an electron with spin $\sigma$ at site $i,$ and $\hat{\boldsymbol n}_{i,\sigma}=\hat{c}_{i,\sigma}^{\dagger}\hat{c}_{i,\sigma}$ is the density operator. The Coulomb interactions are solved using the Hartree mean-field approximation. The substrate is described by the term
$$
\hat{H}_{\mathrm{sub}}=\sum_{\mathbf{k},a,\sigma}\epsilon_{a}(\mathbf{k})\hat{\psi}_{\mathbf{k},a,\sigma}^{\dagger}\hat{\psi}_{\mathbf{k},a,\sigma},
$$
where $\hat{\psi}_{\mathbf{k},a,\sigma}^{\dagger}$ creates an electron with spin $\sigma$ and momentum $\mathbf{k}$ as a plane wave in substrate band $a$ with dispersion $\epsilon_{a}(\mathbf{k})$ . We derive the substrate dispersions from DFT calculations on a substrate slab. The coupling between the substrate and kagome lattice is given by
$$
\hat{H}_{\mathrm{couple}}=V\sum_{i,a}(\hat{c}_{i}^{\dagger}\hat{e}_{{\bf r}_{i},a}+\hat{e}_{{\bf r}_{i},a}^{\dagger}\hat{c}_{i}),
$$
